# Owner-supplied icon-picker template

## What the scaffold installs

Every application scaffold expands the verified picker core into:

```text
public/assets/pickers/icon-picker/
  assets/lucide/       1,952 local SVG files
  assets/twemoji/      3,720 local SVG files
  licenses/            upstream license texts
  src/                 picker, loaders, manifests, and SVG sanitizer
  icon-picker.css
  LICENSES.md
```

The source is the owner-supplied picker extracted from GPT-Mermaid. The foundation
archive retains its complete 5,695-file source snapshot. `README.md` and `demo.html`
remain in that provenance archive but are deliberately not expanded into the public
application: the legacy demo contains optional remote-font requests and demonstrates
behavior that predates the current owner rules.

Approved source versions are Lucide Static 1.14.0, Twemoji SVG 15.0.0,
unicode-emoji-json 0.9.0, and DOMPurify 3.4.7. Do not replace or upgrade them without a
measured compatibility task and a regenerated integrity manifest.

## What travels to a designer

The full packs above are an engineering-project asset library. They do not all travel.
Each prepared designer stage contains:

1. every Lucide component imported by the designer-owned JSX;
2. every Twemoji literal or picker-asset reference found in the designer surface;
3. every dynamic Lucide, Twemoji, or custom SVG selection declared in
   `designer-assets.json`;
4. exactly 30 governed Lucide picker samples and 30 governed Twemoji picker samples;
   and
5. the picker runtime, sanitizer, styles, and required license files.

The used and sample sets are merged without duplicate files. `TRAVELLING-ASSETS.json`
labels every item by reason, byte count, path, and SHA-256. It also says explicitly
that the picker is a limited sample, never the full library. Missing declared assets,
unknown names, source/manifest disagreement, extra picker files, or hash drift block
stage preparation.

When a selected asset lives in application data rather than visible JSX/CSS, update
`designer-assets.json` as part of the same implementation change. Do not wait for a
designer prompt and do not add the full pack to avoid maintaining that declaration.
The stage's generated Lucide React global likewise contains only components actually
imported by the UI.

## Template, not automatic permission

Read owner rules D2.1 through D2.12 before using this component. Having the Twemoji
pack and an Emoji tab in the raw source is asset capability; it is not permission to
show emoji in a product. Ordinary UI chrome remains Lucide-only. Country flags use
local Twemoji under D2.4. Any other emoji use needs a scoped owner request.

Product code must not import the raw picker directly. Create one project-owned adapter
as the only import boundary and adapt the supplied component before first use. The
adapter must:

1. expose only icon types authorized for that exact surface;
2. map the picker `--ip-*` hooks to the product's dark-first design tokens;
3. retain the supplied DOMPurify SVG sanitization and local asset resolution;
4. replace native `title`-only hints with the product's designed tooltip component;
5. replace `?`, native-glyph, swallowed-callback, and other silent fallback behavior
   with actionable errors and general-rule G2.8 logging when degradation is approved;
6. await durable host persistence and keep the dialog open on failure;
7. restore focus, implement the required keyboard/dialog semantics, and route any
   destructive action through a designed confirmation; and
8. preserve the bundled license texts and Twemoji attribution.

Before starting the BlackBox task, trace the complete integration surface: the raw
picker entry, project adapter, launcher, containing dialog or popover, portal/layer
host, product styles, persistence callback, unit tests, and Chrome path. Put every
foreseeable project-owned path in the initial task contract. Do not begin with only
the launcher and discover the adapter or layer path after editing. A genuinely
unforeseeable path still follows BlackBox's owner-approved amendment rule.

These are known adaptation points in the supplied source, not permission to discard
the source and invent another picker. Change the smallest project-owned surface needed,
keep its API recognizable, and prove the adapted behavior in visible independent
Chrome.

## Native modal and top-layer integration

The provenance-locked raw picker mounts `.cat-editor-backdrop` under `document.body`.
A native `<dialog>` opened with `showModal()` occupies the browser top layer. In that
composition, the picker can exist in the DOM yet be hidden behind the dialog and
receive no clicks.

The scaffold provides:

```text
app/src/ui/owner-picker-layer.js
tests/owner-picker-layer.test.js
```

The product adapter must capture the launcher and containing dialog before opening
the raw picker, then place and prove the raw backdrop immediately:

```js
import {
  assertOwnerPickerInteractable,
  placeOwnerPickerBackdrop,
} from "./owner-picker-layer.js";

const origin = document.activeElement;
rawPicker.openIconPicker(options);
const backdrop = document.querySelector(".cat-editor-backdrop");
const placement = placeOwnerPickerBackdrop({
  backdrop,
  origin,
  layerRoot: settingsDialogRef.current,
});

requestAnimationFrame(() => {
  assertOwnerPickerInteractable(backdrop);
});
```

When no native modal is active, the helper retains the raw document-level mount.
When a native modal is active, it fails unless the adapter identifies that front
layer; it never silently leaves the picker behind it. Observe the returned
`placement.host` (or the whole document subtree) for subsequent raw-picker mutations.
On placement or hit-test failure, close the raw picker, preserve the user's unsaved
selection, restore focus, and show the exact layer failure. Do not downgrade the
failure to a body-level fallback.

Visible Chrome proof must invoke the picker through the real launcher and show:

1. a non-zero rendered picker rectangle;
2. native center-point hit testing that returns the picker or its descendant;
3. pointer and keyboard selection, Tab containment, Escape/cancel, and focus return;
4. no console error, warning, DOM-nesting warning, or unhandled rejection;
5. stable outer geometry across tabs and expected picker choices; and
6. no document-level horizontal overflow at every supported narrow viewport,
   including 390 CSS pixels when mobile is supported.

DOM presence, a unit test of the helper, or a screenshot without interaction is not
this proof.

## Runtime boundary

The unbundled ES modules and their assets are served from the same local directory.
The raw entry point is:

```text
/assets/pickers/icon-picker/src/index.js
```

The raw stylesheet is:

```text
/assets/pickers/icon-picker/icon-picker.css
```

Only the project adapter may import that entry point. If a bundler relocates the
module, call the supplied `configureIconPicker` with the local
`/assets/pickers/icon-picker/assets/` base. No CDN fallback is permitted.

For a country selector, derive the Twemoji key from the real ISO country code through
the supplied loader and render the returned local SVG. If it does not resolve, show a
specific error naming the safe country code, the local asset operation, and why the
flag was required. Do not fall back to the operating system's Unicode flag glyph.

## Attribution

Keep `LICENSES.md` and all four files under `licenses/`. Twemoji artwork requires
visible attribution in application documentation or an About/Credits surface:

> Emoji artwork by Twemoji (Copyright Twitter, Inc and other contributors), licensed
> under CC-BY 4.0. Icons by Lucide, licensed under ISC.

## Verification

The project scaffold record lists every expanded file with its byte count and SHA-256.
The foundation checks also prove the archive hash, sorted safe paths, complete source
inventory, selected public inventory, Lucide count, Twemoji count, required files, and
license markers. Stage verification separately proves the reduced carried inventory.
A mismatch blocks scaffolding or stage preparation before anything is shipped.
